WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.800 --> 00:00:15.880
MODERATOR: Thank you
all for joining us

00:00:15.880 --> 00:00:18.480
in the privilege of
being present today

00:00:18.480 --> 00:00:22.200
to celebrate this wonderful
occasion in the life

00:00:22.200 --> 00:00:24.660
of science, and of
MIT, and certainly

00:00:24.660 --> 00:00:27.360
of our friend and
colleague Bob Horvitz.

00:00:27.360 --> 00:00:34.080
There is no event that more
symbolizes and reminds us

00:00:34.080 --> 00:00:38.010
of why we are all here than
when one of our colleagues

00:00:38.010 --> 00:00:41.580
wins the highest possible
accolade for the science

00:00:41.580 --> 00:00:44.010
that he or she has done.

00:00:44.010 --> 00:00:46.710
The things that we
do at MIT, the things

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:51.930
that people, especially like
Bob, do in basic research,

00:00:51.930 --> 00:00:54.240
in my view, is at
the absolute highest

00:00:54.240 --> 00:00:56.910
level of human accomplishment.

00:00:56.910 --> 00:01:01.470
And it is truly a privilege
to count Bob among our friends

00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:05.850
and colleagues, and to
recognize how he has advanced

00:01:05.850 --> 00:01:07.950
the state of human knowledge.

00:01:07.950 --> 00:01:12.150
And how that knowledge
ultimately will improve health

00:01:12.150 --> 00:01:13.500
for humankind.

00:01:13.500 --> 00:01:15.280
So thank you all for being here.

00:01:15.280 --> 00:01:19.260
And I would like now to ask
our colleague, Phil Sharpe,

00:01:19.260 --> 00:01:19.980
to introduce Bob.

00:01:19.980 --> 00:01:20.480
Phil.

00:01:30.090 --> 00:01:32.130
SHARPE: It's a great
honor to be asked

00:01:32.130 --> 00:01:35.190
to introduce Robert Horvitz.

00:01:35.190 --> 00:01:38.850
And I want to begin
by congratulating

00:01:38.850 --> 00:01:43.650
Bob for his outstanding
research and work here

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:45.660
at MIT as a colleague.

00:01:45.660 --> 00:01:47.370
Bob's research,
as you will hear,

00:01:47.370 --> 00:01:50.190
is fundamental to
all life processes.

00:01:50.190 --> 00:01:52.770
Fundamental to how a
multicellular organism

00:01:52.770 --> 00:01:57.810
is formed, fundamental to how
our brain is formed and works,

00:01:57.810 --> 00:02:00.195
and fundamental to
the processes related

00:02:00.195 --> 00:02:02.550
to disease such as cancer.

00:02:02.550 --> 00:02:05.940
And therefore, as you would
expect, Bob has many homes.

00:02:05.940 --> 00:02:08.970
He is a professor in the
Department of Biology,

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:12.240
he is an investigator in the
McGovern Institute for Brain

00:02:12.240 --> 00:02:17.370
Research, and he is a member of
the Center for Cancer Research

00:02:17.370 --> 00:02:19.980
here at MIT.

00:02:19.980 --> 00:02:24.180
Beyond the borders of MIT,
Bob has also other homes.

00:02:24.180 --> 00:02:27.810
He is a investigator in
the Howard Hughes Medical

00:02:27.810 --> 00:02:31.200
Institute, and has been
supported by Howard Hughes

00:02:31.200 --> 00:02:32.730
for many years.

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:35.700
And from the day he
came to MIT, almost,

00:02:35.700 --> 00:02:39.210
he has been supported by the
National Institute of Health.

00:02:39.210 --> 00:02:43.180
So Bob has many
friends and many homes.

00:02:43.180 --> 00:02:45.820
Now, it has been
said that the best

00:02:45.820 --> 00:02:49.930
predictor of whether you're
going to receive a Nobel Prize,

00:02:49.930 --> 00:02:54.730
statistically, is having
your office within 50 yards

00:02:54.730 --> 00:02:56.110
of another Nobel Prize winner.

00:02:59.360 --> 00:03:05.120
In Bob's case, you want to
interpret that as mentors.

00:03:05.120 --> 00:03:07.490
Now Bob was an
undergrad at MIT, where

00:03:07.490 --> 00:03:12.230
he majored in mathematics
and economics,

00:03:12.230 --> 00:03:17.030
and his advisor here was Bob
Solow, who, if you don't know,

00:03:17.030 --> 00:03:19.680
has a Nobel in economics.

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:23.190
He went to Harvard,
and his advisor

00:03:23.190 --> 00:03:26.070
at Harvard, where he
got his PhD in biology

00:03:26.070 --> 00:03:31.350
was Jim Watson
and Wally Gilbert.

00:03:31.350 --> 00:03:35.400
And that's three for three.

00:03:35.400 --> 00:03:39.030
And then he went to
Cambridge to do a post-doc.

00:03:39.030 --> 00:03:42.720
And there, things were
getting a little slow for him

00:03:42.720 --> 00:03:45.090
in keeping this perfect record.

00:03:45.090 --> 00:03:49.170
And his mentor at Cambridge
was Sidney Brenner

00:03:49.170 --> 00:03:50.940
and John Sulston.

00:03:50.940 --> 00:03:52.680
Sidney was his
post-doctoral mentor,

00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.970
John is who he worked with, and
they share this honor with him

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:58.420
this year.

00:03:58.420 --> 00:04:03.057
And that makes it
5 for five for Bob.

00:04:03.057 --> 00:04:05.640
So if he wants to be a post-doc
in somebody's lab around here,

00:04:05.640 --> 00:04:10.500
I'm sure others
would accept him.

00:04:10.500 --> 00:04:15.360
Bob has been at MIT for many
years, since I think '76,

00:04:15.360 --> 00:04:17.160
or something like that.

00:04:17.160 --> 00:04:20.370
He not only has done
outstanding research,

00:04:20.370 --> 00:04:24.090
but he has been a
wonderful colleague.

00:04:24.090 --> 00:04:25.080
He teaches.

00:04:25.080 --> 00:04:28.050
He teaches remarkably
well, as you will learn,

00:04:28.050 --> 00:04:29.730
and enthusiastically.

00:04:29.730 --> 00:04:31.680
And he has trained
some of the best

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.080
scientists this country has.

00:04:34.080 --> 00:04:36.570
Every major university
in this country

00:04:36.570 --> 00:04:38.790
has someone who
has been trained,

00:04:38.790 --> 00:04:42.120
either as a graduate student
or a post-doctoral fellow,

00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:44.220
in Bob Horvitz's lab.

00:04:44.220 --> 00:04:47.700
He is the professional constant.

00:04:47.700 --> 00:04:51.000
So it's a great pleasure
to introduce Bob today.

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:52.770
Let's all give him
around of applause.

00:05:30.380 --> 00:05:35.030
HORVITZ: Thank you, Chuck and
Phil, for the introduction.

00:05:35.030 --> 00:05:48.620
And thank all of you
[LAUGHING] including my wife

00:05:48.620 --> 00:05:54.800
Martha, for coming here today.

00:05:59.420 --> 00:06:03.120
This is a very
exciting time for me

00:06:03.120 --> 00:06:06.180
and I can think of nothing
better than to share

00:06:06.180 --> 00:06:10.170
this time with my MIT friends.

00:06:10.170 --> 00:06:14.800
And I'd like to start with a few
specific statements of thanks

00:06:14.800 --> 00:06:16.770
to these friends.

00:06:16.770 --> 00:06:20.680
First and foremost, to my lab.

00:06:20.680 --> 00:06:22.450
I thank the students
and postdocs

00:06:22.450 --> 00:06:25.720
who have been members
of our research group,

00:06:25.720 --> 00:06:30.250
because, as I'm sure most
people in this audience know,

00:06:30.250 --> 00:06:34.480
it's really their
efforts, their insights,

00:06:34.480 --> 00:06:38.590
and their accomplishments,
that have been recognized.

00:06:38.590 --> 00:06:42.136
I thank my colleagues in
the Department of Biology.

00:06:42.136 --> 00:06:45.980
These colleagues have provided
a spectacularly supportive

00:06:45.980 --> 00:06:49.520
environment, and have
fostered my growth

00:06:49.520 --> 00:06:52.240
as an independent scientist.

00:06:52.240 --> 00:06:55.030
And their help,
their friendship,

00:06:55.030 --> 00:07:01.020
remains a major force behind
my scientific efforts.

00:07:01.020 --> 00:07:05.360
I particularly thank Boris
Magasanik, who hired me.

00:07:07.950 --> 00:07:11.220
And also my other ex-
and current department

00:07:11.220 --> 00:07:19.200
heads, Jean Brown, Morie Fox,
Richard Hines, Phil Sharpe,

00:07:19.200 --> 00:07:24.840
and Bob Sour, for their
support over many years.

00:07:24.840 --> 00:07:27.720
I thank Bob Sour
and Graham Walker,

00:07:27.720 --> 00:07:29.190
with whom I grew up at MIT.

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:35.210
We entered as faculty members
at very much the same time.

00:07:35.210 --> 00:07:41.740
And I thank Salva Luria and
David Baltimore, both of whom

00:07:41.740 --> 00:07:46.660
made me think about
science, but also made

00:07:46.660 --> 00:07:53.230
me think beyond science, to the
responsibilities of a scientist

00:07:53.230 --> 00:07:57.300
in the country and the world.

00:07:57.300 --> 00:08:03.860
Now Phil, when I first
came to MIT, it was 1964.

00:08:03.860 --> 00:08:06.360
I'm kind of homegrown.

00:08:06.360 --> 00:08:08.520
I came in 1964 as a freshman.

00:08:08.520 --> 00:08:12.810
At that time, Paul Gray
was a 32-year-old associate

00:08:12.810 --> 00:08:16.020
professor, and if
I recall correctly,

00:08:16.020 --> 00:08:19.440
he was then, or soon thereafter,
chair of the freshman

00:08:19.440 --> 00:08:21.760
advisory council.

00:08:21.760 --> 00:08:25.720
The associate dean of
students was Fred Facet.

00:08:25.720 --> 00:08:28.810
And I remember Fred very
distinctly from day one,

00:08:28.810 --> 00:08:33.370
because as a freshman, I
sat in Kresge auditorium

00:08:33.370 --> 00:08:34.419
as he addressed us.

00:08:34.419 --> 00:08:38.980
And what he said was, look to
your left, look to your right.

00:08:38.980 --> 00:08:42.370
One of you won't be here
when graduation time comes.

00:08:47.800 --> 00:08:52.510
Times have changed and
some of us are still here.

00:08:56.730 --> 00:09:03.560
In this room I took 601,
Introductory Electrical

00:09:03.560 --> 00:09:07.220
Engineering from Amar Bose.

00:09:07.220 --> 00:09:12.530
Most of you probably know
him from speakers, radios,

00:09:12.530 --> 00:09:15.030
and headsets.

00:09:15.030 --> 00:09:17.960
He knows electrical engineering.

00:09:17.960 --> 00:09:20.270
As Phil said, I
graduated with degrees

00:09:20.270 --> 00:09:25.410
in, well he didn't quite
say it this way, 18 and 14.

00:09:25.410 --> 00:09:28.030
Mathematics and economics.

00:09:28.030 --> 00:09:32.110
And, as Phil also noted, I
had the wonderful opportunity

00:09:32.110 --> 00:09:35.890
to work with Bob Solow for
my undergraduate thesis

00:09:35.890 --> 00:09:38.410
in economics.

00:09:38.410 --> 00:09:41.700
The time then was
in the late 60s.

00:09:41.700 --> 00:09:45.700
And I wanted to do
something different.

00:09:45.700 --> 00:09:50.700
And that something
turned out to be biology.

00:09:50.700 --> 00:09:55.930
So what I would like to do now
is to turn to that biology.

00:09:55.930 --> 00:10:00.970
The 2002 Nobel Prize in
physiology or medicine

00:10:00.970 --> 00:10:06.370
has been awarded to Sidney
Brenner, John Sulston, and me,

00:10:06.370 --> 00:10:08.950
for a set of
interrelated studies

00:10:08.950 --> 00:10:14.900
that focus around discoveries
concerning a phenomenon known

00:10:14.900 --> 00:10:18.590
as programmed cell death.

00:10:18.590 --> 00:10:23.120
Why should anyone care
about dying cells?

00:10:23.120 --> 00:10:28.480
What does it mean that
certain deaths are programmed?

00:10:28.480 --> 00:10:32.950
First, let me say that it is
not notable that cells die.

00:10:32.950 --> 00:10:35.500
This has been known for
many years by biologists.

00:10:35.500 --> 00:10:37.150
In fact, the real
problem has been

00:10:37.150 --> 00:10:40.660
that in order to study cells
one has to keep them alive.

00:10:40.660 --> 00:10:43.750
When they die that's not
interesting, that's a problem.

00:10:47.420 --> 00:10:53.190
What is notable is that, in
many cases, when cells die,

00:10:53.190 --> 00:10:55.640
the cell death is
a manifestation

00:10:55.640 --> 00:10:57.290
of a normal process.

00:10:57.290 --> 00:10:59.420
More specifically,
what's happening

00:10:59.420 --> 00:11:01.280
is that cells die
not because they've

00:11:01.280 --> 00:11:07.760
been damaged beyond life, or
in some terrible environment,

00:11:07.760 --> 00:11:13.550
but rather because they have
activated an endogenous program

00:11:13.550 --> 00:11:16.630
of cellular suicide.

00:11:16.630 --> 00:11:20.740
Now what about the term
programmed cell death?

00:11:20.740 --> 00:11:24.760
To me, programmed cell death
is synonymous with naturally

00:11:24.760 --> 00:11:26.830
occurring cell death.

00:11:26.830 --> 00:11:29.380
And let me explain
what I mean by that.

00:11:29.380 --> 00:11:32.230
During the development of an
animal from a single cell,

00:11:32.230 --> 00:11:37.720
from a fertilized egg,
many things happen.

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:40.300
First, the egg divides.

00:11:40.300 --> 00:11:44.200
Over and over and
over again, generating

00:11:44.200 --> 00:11:45.820
a large number of cells.

00:11:45.820 --> 00:11:48.190
For example, in us, we
don't know exactly what

00:11:48.190 --> 00:11:50.750
that number is, but it's
probably on the order of 10

00:11:50.750 --> 00:11:51.750
to the 13th.

00:11:51.750 --> 00:11:53.380
10 million million.

00:11:53.380 --> 00:11:55.810
And then each of these
cells must differentiate.

00:11:55.810 --> 00:11:58.450
They must take on
specific characteristics

00:11:58.450 --> 00:12:00.130
and become certain
types of cells,

00:12:00.130 --> 00:12:04.330
like a nerve cell, or a
muscle cell, or a skin cell.

00:12:04.330 --> 00:12:06.640
And then all of these
cells must interact

00:12:06.640 --> 00:12:12.850
as groups to form proper
structures, an arm, or a liver,

00:12:12.850 --> 00:12:16.450
or whatever, and
proper interactions,

00:12:16.450 --> 00:12:20.350
proper interconnections,
as in the most complicated

00:12:20.350 --> 00:12:24.020
of organs, the brain.

00:12:24.020 --> 00:12:27.890
These processes of cell
division, cell differentiation,

00:12:27.890 --> 00:12:32.840
and morphogenesis constitute
basic events of development,

00:12:32.840 --> 00:12:39.090
and they define basic problems
in developmental biology.

00:12:39.090 --> 00:12:42.300
But now, in addition
to these processes,

00:12:42.300 --> 00:12:46.290
there's another process
that appears to be universal

00:12:46.290 --> 00:12:48.660
among developing animals.

00:12:48.660 --> 00:12:52.540
And that is the
process of cell death.

00:12:52.540 --> 00:12:55.330
Quite remarkably,
many of the cells

00:12:55.330 --> 00:12:58.180
that are generated
as animals develop

00:12:58.180 --> 00:13:01.810
do not survive, but instead die.

00:13:01.810 --> 00:13:05.230
And it's this naturally
occurring cell death

00:13:05.230 --> 00:13:08.290
that is often referred
to as programmed cell

00:13:08.290 --> 00:13:13.030
death, because it is an
aspect of the natural quote

00:13:13.030 --> 00:13:17.180
"program" of development.

00:13:17.180 --> 00:13:19.990
Now, as biologists have learned
more about programmed cell

00:13:19.990 --> 00:13:23.080
death, the term
has been extended

00:13:23.080 --> 00:13:27.910
to other deaths that use
the same mechanisms as those

00:13:27.910 --> 00:13:30.980
that occur normally
during development.

00:13:30.980 --> 00:13:34.540
So, certain cell deaths that
biologists study in vitro,

00:13:34.540 --> 00:13:36.880
In the test tube
or on Petri plates,

00:13:36.880 --> 00:13:39.400
are programmed cell deaths.

00:13:39.400 --> 00:13:42.670
And in some diseases
what one sees

00:13:42.670 --> 00:13:46.480
are programmed cell deaths.

00:13:46.480 --> 00:13:48.970
Now, that cell death occurs
during the normal course

00:13:48.970 --> 00:13:51.400
of animal development has
been known for many years

00:13:51.400 --> 00:13:53.560
by developmental biologists.

00:13:53.560 --> 00:13:59.640
For example, the regression
of the tail of a tadpole,

00:13:59.640 --> 00:14:03.120
as a tadpole becomes a frog,
involves the programed deaths

00:14:03.120 --> 00:14:07.440
of essentially all of the cell
types in the tadpole tail.

00:14:07.440 --> 00:14:09.960
Similarly, the
formation of digits,

00:14:09.960 --> 00:14:15.510
for us fingers and
toes, that formation

00:14:15.510 --> 00:14:20.400
involves the elimination
of interdigital regions

00:14:20.400 --> 00:14:24.330
that are cellular,
in us, in utero.

00:14:24.330 --> 00:14:29.700
And these regions are removed
by programmed cell death.

00:14:29.700 --> 00:14:32.140
We can see this
exemplified in birds.

00:14:32.140 --> 00:14:35.460
If you think about birds,
think about bird feet,

00:14:35.460 --> 00:14:38.770
some are webbed,
and some are not.

00:14:38.770 --> 00:14:42.330
And here we see some
friends, MIT friends.

00:14:42.330 --> 00:14:46.020
This is from the food
court, this pigeon.

00:14:46.020 --> 00:14:49.800
There near all the
trucks, near building 68.

00:14:49.800 --> 00:14:52.500
This is Charles River Goose.

00:14:52.500 --> 00:14:54.630
Webbed, not webbed.

00:14:54.630 --> 00:14:57.970
Programmed cell death,
no programmed cell death,

00:14:57.970 --> 00:14:58.470
in the foot.

00:15:01.370 --> 00:15:04.070
Programmed cell death
is not a minor event.

00:15:04.070 --> 00:15:07.610
For example, approximately
95% of the thymus sites,

00:15:07.610 --> 00:15:13.850
blood cells, in our immune
system, 95% that are generated

00:15:13.850 --> 00:15:16.200
die by programmed cell death.

00:15:16.200 --> 00:15:21.050
In areas of the developing
mammalian brain, as many as 85%

00:15:21.050 --> 00:15:24.965
of the nerve cells die
by programmed cell death.

00:15:24.965 --> 00:15:29.420
Yet, despite this very
pervasive phenomenon,

00:15:29.420 --> 00:15:33.620
it was ignored for many
years by biologists.

00:15:33.620 --> 00:15:34.370
Why is that?

00:15:34.370 --> 00:15:36.690
Well, I think there are
a number of reasons.

00:15:36.690 --> 00:15:38.270
One, I already alluded to.

00:15:38.270 --> 00:15:41.660
But another has to do with
timing, with kinetics.

00:15:41.660 --> 00:15:46.340
And that is, that this process
of cell death is quite rapid.

00:15:46.340 --> 00:15:50.000
So if one takes a snapshot, by
looking at a sample of tissue,

00:15:50.000 --> 00:15:53.720
and says, how many cells
are in the throes of death?

00:15:53.720 --> 00:15:55.400
The answer is very few.

00:15:55.400 --> 00:15:57.600
It's only if you look over time.

00:15:57.600 --> 00:16:00.680
And that's something that one
must know to look for in order

00:16:00.680 --> 00:16:01.580
to assess it.

00:16:06.240 --> 00:16:09.360
To me, perhaps the most
interesting and fundamental

00:16:09.360 --> 00:16:12.450
aspect of programmed
cell death is simply

00:16:12.450 --> 00:16:13.950
what I've said already.

00:16:13.950 --> 00:16:17.570
And that is that there is
a biology of cell death.

00:16:20.260 --> 00:16:24.010
And I think the major finding
in the field of cell death

00:16:24.010 --> 00:16:28.440
is the fact that cell death is
a normal biological process,

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:34.050
and it is an active process
on the part of cells that die.

00:16:34.050 --> 00:16:36.750
The key studies that
led to this conclusion

00:16:36.750 --> 00:16:39.720
demonstrated that
specific genes must

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:43.180
function for programmed
cell death to occur.

00:16:43.180 --> 00:16:46.930
And many of these studies were
performed in my laboratory

00:16:46.930 --> 00:16:49.390
here at MIT.

00:16:49.390 --> 00:16:51.120
And these studies
indicated that there

00:16:51.120 --> 00:16:53.530
is a biology of cell
death, every bit as much

00:16:53.530 --> 00:16:57.730
as there is a biology of other
basic cellular processes,

00:16:57.730 --> 00:17:00.460
like cell division,
cell migration, cell

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:02.670
differentiation.

00:17:02.670 --> 00:17:04.990
OK, so now, I've been
talking about the biology

00:17:04.990 --> 00:17:06.650
of cell death.

00:17:06.650 --> 00:17:11.510
And if you think about it,
any biology can go wrong.

00:17:11.510 --> 00:17:16.099
And if it does so, in us,
that can lead to disease.

00:17:16.099 --> 00:17:19.069
And programmed cell death
is no exception to that.

00:17:19.069 --> 00:17:23.599
It appears that some, and maybe
a very substantial number,

00:17:23.599 --> 00:17:26.540
of human diseases
are consequences

00:17:26.540 --> 00:17:30.752
of disruptions in the control
of programmed cell death.

00:17:30.752 --> 00:17:33.040
For example, the major
clinical features

00:17:33.040 --> 00:17:36.310
of many neurologic disorders,
the neurodegenerative diseases

00:17:36.310 --> 00:17:39.670
include Alzheimer's,
Huntington's, Parkinson's, ALS,

00:17:39.670 --> 00:17:42.790
other disorders, stroke,
traumatic brain injury.

00:17:42.790 --> 00:17:50.820
In all of these cases, there are
cells that die that shouldn't.

00:17:50.820 --> 00:17:53.360
For example, in Lou
Gehrig's disease,

00:17:53.360 --> 00:17:57.600
ALS, amyotrophic lateral
sclerosis, specific nerve cell

00:17:57.600 --> 00:17:59.440
types, the so-called
motor neurons,

00:17:59.440 --> 00:18:02.610
the cells that drive the
contractions, contractions

00:18:02.610 --> 00:18:04.680
of our muscles.

00:18:04.680 --> 00:18:06.690
They die in the
brain spinal cord,

00:18:06.690 --> 00:18:10.470
leading to paralysis,
and eventually to death.

00:18:10.470 --> 00:18:13.590
What causes these
cells to die in ALS

00:18:13.590 --> 00:18:16.050
and other types of
nerve cells to die

00:18:16.050 --> 00:18:18.930
and other
neurodegenerative diseases?

00:18:18.930 --> 00:18:24.020
Let me say, very
specifically, we don't know.

00:18:24.020 --> 00:18:28.190
But, one hypothesis
is that at least some

00:18:28.190 --> 00:18:31.160
of the cell deaths
in these disorders

00:18:31.160 --> 00:18:33.930
are ectopic
programmed cell death.

00:18:33.930 --> 00:18:36.970
In other words, the cells die
by using that same mechanism

00:18:36.970 --> 00:18:39.860
that's used developmentally.

00:18:39.860 --> 00:18:44.080
There is a range of
data that support this

00:18:44.080 --> 00:18:47.340
to varying degrees in
these various disorders,

00:18:47.340 --> 00:18:49.660
and in at least one
neurodegenerative disorder,

00:18:49.660 --> 00:18:54.190
retinal degeneration, I would
say the data are very strong.

00:18:54.190 --> 00:18:56.290
And if you ask me
to guess, I would

00:18:56.290 --> 00:18:58.600
guess that this process
is involved in others

00:18:58.600 --> 00:19:01.440
of these disorders as well.

00:19:01.440 --> 00:19:05.240
So these are disorders in which
there are too much cell death.

00:19:05.240 --> 00:19:09.050
But there also are disorders
of the opposite sort,

00:19:09.050 --> 00:19:14.070
in which there is too little
programmed cell death.

00:19:14.070 --> 00:19:17.930
So that, for example,
cancer, we generally

00:19:17.930 --> 00:19:21.650
think about cancer as a loss
of control over cell division.

00:19:21.650 --> 00:19:23.300
So too many cells are generated.

00:19:23.300 --> 00:19:26.300
And that's a very appropriate
way to think about cancer,

00:19:26.300 --> 00:19:31.570
but it's not the only source
of cancer, ultimately.

00:19:31.570 --> 00:19:35.490
If you think about the number
of cells in our tissues,

00:19:35.490 --> 00:19:37.590
it turns out that
this number is defined

00:19:37.590 --> 00:19:41.740
by two opposing processes,
the process of cell division,

00:19:41.740 --> 00:19:45.030
which adds cells, and the
process of programmed cell

00:19:45.030 --> 00:19:48.030
death, which takes them away.

00:19:48.030 --> 00:19:50.310
That number can
increase, either because

00:19:50.310 --> 00:19:52.800
of too much cell
division, or because

00:19:52.800 --> 00:19:55.140
of too little cell death.

00:19:55.140 --> 00:19:57.930
And certain cancers, the one
studied in the most detail

00:19:57.930 --> 00:20:01.140
is follicular lymphoma,
in fact are cancers

00:20:01.140 --> 00:20:03.480
that ultimately are
caused because there

00:20:03.480 --> 00:20:07.632
is too little cell death.

00:20:07.632 --> 00:20:09.090
So what I noted
earlier is that one

00:20:09.090 --> 00:20:11.610
of the major reasons for
concluding that cell death is

00:20:11.610 --> 00:20:14.910
an active biological process
is the finding that there

00:20:14.910 --> 00:20:17.190
are specific genes involved.

00:20:17.190 --> 00:20:19.230
And what I'm going to
do now is to tell you

00:20:19.230 --> 00:20:21.570
about such genes,
based upon the work

00:20:21.570 --> 00:20:24.800
that we've done here
in our laboratory.

00:20:24.800 --> 00:20:27.500
And we've discovered such genes
not by studying cell deaths

00:20:27.500 --> 00:20:29.730
in humans, or human disease.

00:20:29.730 --> 00:20:31.710
Humans are complicated,
and not ideal

00:20:31.710 --> 00:20:35.010
experimental organisms
for many purposes.

00:20:35.010 --> 00:20:39.450
But rather what we studied
is a microscopic roundworm,

00:20:39.450 --> 00:20:45.060
or nematode, caenorhabditis
elegans, or c. elegans

00:20:45.060 --> 00:20:48.300
Now, if you ask about
this year's Nobel Prize,

00:20:48.300 --> 00:20:52.070
the major player is
this little worm,

00:20:52.070 --> 00:20:56.400
c. elegans Because,
all three of us,

00:20:56.400 --> 00:20:58.920
Sydney Brenner, John
Sulston, and myself,

00:20:58.920 --> 00:21:05.500
are being honored because of
studies of this little worm.

00:21:05.500 --> 00:21:10.490
This is Sydney, often referred
to as the father of the worm.

00:21:13.610 --> 00:21:16.460
Sydney is a brilliant scientist.

00:21:16.460 --> 00:21:19.440
Sydney helped pioneer the
field of molecular genetics.

00:21:19.440 --> 00:21:21.320
He had a major role
in the elucidation

00:21:21.320 --> 00:21:25.640
of the genetic code, he was a
discoverer of messenger RNA,

00:21:25.640 --> 00:21:29.480
and he has countless
other major discoveries.

00:21:29.480 --> 00:21:32.240
Sydney was drawn to
c. elegans in part,

00:21:32.240 --> 00:21:33.440
because it's very simple.

00:21:33.440 --> 00:21:38.180
We now know that there are only
959 cells in the c. elegans

00:21:38.180 --> 00:21:41.440
adult. And this can contrast
with the 10th to the 13th

00:21:41.440 --> 00:21:45.320
and in humans, or something
in the hundreds of thousands

00:21:45.320 --> 00:21:47.150
to millions in insects.

00:21:47.150 --> 00:21:49.170
959.

00:21:49.170 --> 00:21:52.580
He was also drawn to c.
elegans because of features

00:21:52.580 --> 00:21:54.990
of its genetics, features that
made it exceptionally well

00:21:54.990 --> 00:21:57.230
suited for genetic studies.

00:21:57.230 --> 00:22:02.000
Geneticists are concerned
with rare individuals, events

00:22:02.000 --> 00:22:04.110
that occur one in a million.

00:22:04.110 --> 00:22:06.950
And so, it's nice
to be able to study

00:22:06.950 --> 00:22:10.640
a million individuals, or
10 million, or 100 million,

00:22:10.640 --> 00:22:12.140
or what have you.

00:22:12.140 --> 00:22:15.320
And with some organisms,
that's very hard.

00:22:15.320 --> 00:22:18.410
With c. elegans, because it's
only one millimeter in length,

00:22:18.410 --> 00:22:21.060
and very easy to grow,
on a single Petri dish

00:22:21.060 --> 00:22:24.380
we can grow 10,000
individuals, and many more

00:22:24.380 --> 00:22:28.670
than that in a liquid culture.

00:22:28.670 --> 00:22:33.110
So the cellular simplicity
and appropriateness

00:22:33.110 --> 00:22:34.160
for genetic studies.

00:22:34.160 --> 00:22:37.340
The other thing I should
note is that c. elegans

00:22:37.340 --> 00:22:38.810
grows very rapidly.

00:22:38.810 --> 00:22:42.420
So geneticists are also
interested in generations.

00:22:42.420 --> 00:22:45.150
You go from one generation,
to the next, to the next.

00:22:45.150 --> 00:22:47.600
C. elegans has a three
day generation time.

00:22:47.600 --> 00:22:49.790
From the time an egg
is produced to the time

00:22:49.790 --> 00:22:52.760
that egg is adult producing
its own eggs is three days.

00:22:52.760 --> 00:22:55.700
Many generations,
lots of animals,

00:22:55.700 --> 00:23:01.620
simple cellular anatomy,
Sydney said worms are good.

00:23:01.620 --> 00:23:04.820
Let's use them for
studies, genetic studies,

00:23:04.820 --> 00:23:08.620
of developmental biology
and neurobiology.

00:23:08.620 --> 00:23:10.925
And he pioneered such analyses.

00:23:13.720 --> 00:23:19.120
John Sulston, the third
of this year's recipients,

00:23:19.120 --> 00:23:23.580
and I both worked in Sydney's
lab at the Medical Research

00:23:23.580 --> 00:23:27.400
Council Laboratory in
England, of molecular biology.

00:23:27.400 --> 00:23:28.440
Cambridge, England.

00:23:28.440 --> 00:23:32.790
Early 1970s, early to mid 1970s.

00:23:32.790 --> 00:23:35.670
I was a post-doctoral fellow.

00:23:35.670 --> 00:23:39.090
I was supported by the
Muscular Dystrophy Association,

00:23:39.090 --> 00:23:41.400
and as Phil noted, I
came from the Harvard

00:23:41.400 --> 00:23:45.200
lab of Jim Watson, Wally
Gilbert, and also Claus Weber.

00:23:45.200 --> 00:23:49.320
And there, I had studied
the bacteria phage T4,

00:23:49.320 --> 00:23:53.890
and what it does to its
host RNA polymerase.

00:23:53.890 --> 00:23:55.860
An interest that
Phil has continued

00:23:55.860 --> 00:23:58.800
with in a broader sense.

00:23:58.800 --> 00:24:02.580
John was a staff
scientist at the MRC.

00:24:02.580 --> 00:24:04.830
He had received his PhD
in organic chemistry

00:24:04.830 --> 00:24:08.490
from the University of Cambridge
and then went to La Jolla

00:24:08.490 --> 00:24:12.870
and worked with Leslie Orgel on
essentially biochemical studies

00:24:12.870 --> 00:24:14.760
of the origin of life.

00:24:14.760 --> 00:24:17.040
And came back to
work with Sydney

00:24:17.040 --> 00:24:21.240
to focus on the
neurochemistry of c. elegans.

00:24:21.240 --> 00:24:23.220
So all three of us were
interested in the worm,

00:24:23.220 --> 00:24:25.980
and in fact all three of us
were really very much interested

00:24:25.980 --> 00:24:30.640
and maintain a strong interest
in the nervous system.

00:24:30.640 --> 00:24:32.390
This is the worm.

00:24:32.390 --> 00:24:33.920
The worm comes in two sexes.

00:24:33.920 --> 00:24:37.160
The lower one is a male,
the top one is not.

00:24:37.160 --> 00:24:39.500
It is a hermaphrodite.

00:24:39.500 --> 00:24:41.540
It's essentially a
female whose gonad,

00:24:41.540 --> 00:24:45.140
for a brief period of time,
forgets and makes sperm.

00:24:45.140 --> 00:24:47.210
That has various
experimental advantages,

00:24:47.210 --> 00:24:50.160
but I won't go into
that right now.

00:24:50.160 --> 00:24:54.120
This image shows photographs
taken by John Sulston

00:24:54.120 --> 00:24:58.040
and diagrams that I drew.

00:24:58.040 --> 00:25:03.380
When I arrived in Cambridge,
that Cambridge, John had just

00:25:03.380 --> 00:25:06.980
discovered that by
observing living c. elegans,

00:25:06.980 --> 00:25:09.920
using a Nomarski optics
under the light microscope,

00:25:09.920 --> 00:25:13.460
he could see individual
cells, and follow them

00:25:13.460 --> 00:25:14.900
as they divided.

00:25:14.900 --> 00:25:17.430
And here's an image from
a paper John and I did.

00:25:17.430 --> 00:25:19.820
If you look at either of
these cells, p9 nine and p10,

00:25:19.820 --> 00:25:21.860
here's a time
course, each of them

00:25:21.860 --> 00:25:24.560
undergoes the process
of mitosis, dividing

00:25:24.560 --> 00:25:26.570
to make two daughter cells.

00:25:26.570 --> 00:25:29.440
Watching life.

00:25:29.440 --> 00:25:32.830
Well, this was pretty amazing.

00:25:32.830 --> 00:25:34.660
You could follow
these cells, and then

00:25:34.660 --> 00:25:38.350
follow what their descendants
did, and they would go on

00:25:38.350 --> 00:25:39.400
and divide.

00:25:39.400 --> 00:25:41.980
And so you could follow a
cell, and its daughters,

00:25:41.980 --> 00:25:44.390
and its daughters, and
so on and so forth.

00:25:44.390 --> 00:25:46.540
And for example, these
two cells, P9 and P10,

00:25:46.540 --> 00:25:48.670
they went on and
divided as shown here,

00:25:48.670 --> 00:25:51.940
to generate ultimately
six descendants each,

00:25:51.940 --> 00:25:55.370
in a very short order of time.

00:25:55.370 --> 00:25:57.460
So in talking about
it, John and I

00:25:57.460 --> 00:26:01.570
decided that it should
be possible to determine

00:26:01.570 --> 00:26:03.170
every cell division
that occurred,

00:26:03.170 --> 00:26:06.220
at least during larval
development after hatching,

00:26:06.220 --> 00:26:07.630
in this animal.

00:26:07.630 --> 00:26:09.820
And with the added efforts
of Judith Kimball, who

00:26:09.820 --> 00:26:13.270
was a graduate student with
David Hirsch in Boulder,

00:26:13.270 --> 00:26:15.850
and she was interested
in gonadal development,

00:26:15.850 --> 00:26:18.790
we did exactly that.

00:26:18.790 --> 00:26:21.460
John and I published
the complete description

00:26:21.460 --> 00:26:24.400
of the non-gonadal post
embryonic cell lineages

00:26:24.400 --> 00:26:28.210
of c. elegans in 1977.

00:26:28.210 --> 00:26:30.820
And two years later, Judith
Kimball and David Hirsch

00:26:30.820 --> 00:26:33.640
published the gonadal
lineages, thereby

00:26:33.640 --> 00:26:38.140
bringing the post embryonic
lineage to completion.

00:26:38.140 --> 00:26:40.420
John then closed the loop.

00:26:40.420 --> 00:26:46.180
He studied the egg, and followed
that first fertilized cell,

00:26:46.180 --> 00:26:48.700
fertilized egg cell, as
it divided and divided,

00:26:48.700 --> 00:26:50.500
ultimately to make the larva.

00:26:50.500 --> 00:26:52.600
Now this was hard.

00:26:52.600 --> 00:26:54.340
Let me just tell
you that this is,

00:26:54.340 --> 00:26:58.480
if you picture a bowl of
grapes with about 550 grapes,

00:26:58.480 --> 00:27:01.060
and your assignment
is to watch all 550

00:27:01.060 --> 00:27:03.610
as somebody is standing there
shaking the bowl, that's

00:27:03.610 --> 00:27:05.860
essentially what John did.

00:27:05.860 --> 00:27:08.470
So my part was easy.

00:27:08.470 --> 00:27:12.640
John did the hard part,
and he did it completely.

00:27:12.640 --> 00:27:16.570
And that led to this,
which is the complete cell

00:27:16.570 --> 00:27:18.550
lineage of the c.
elegans hermaphrodite.

00:27:18.550 --> 00:27:21.010
We also have the male, looks
a little bit different.

00:27:21.010 --> 00:27:22.960
Starting with a single
self-fertilized egg,

00:27:22.960 --> 00:27:25.570
divides to make two, divides
again, so on and so forth,

00:27:25.570 --> 00:27:27.160
y-axis is y.

00:27:27.160 --> 00:27:30.880
And that's the developmental
origin of all 959 nuclei

00:27:30.880 --> 00:27:33.670
that are found in the adult.

00:27:33.670 --> 00:27:36.979
This pattern, and the
corresponding pattern

00:27:36.979 --> 00:27:38.770
of the fates of these
cells, is essentially

00:27:38.770 --> 00:27:41.350
invariant from animal to animal.

00:27:41.350 --> 00:27:45.160
There is enormous biologic
information in the cell

00:27:45.160 --> 00:27:48.340
lineage, but I won't
go into that today,

00:27:48.340 --> 00:27:51.520
except to say that the
knowledge of the cell lineage

00:27:51.520 --> 00:27:55.060
has made possible the
analysis of the mechanisms

00:27:55.060 --> 00:27:57.160
responsible for the
development of this animal

00:27:57.160 --> 00:28:01.220
at the level of resolution
of single cells.

00:28:01.220 --> 00:28:04.100
Now, in addition
to the 959 cells

00:28:04.100 --> 00:28:06.050
that are found in the
adult, this cell lineage

00:28:06.050 --> 00:28:12.350
generates another 131 cells, not
found in the adult. The reason?

00:28:12.350 --> 00:28:15.290
They undergo
programmed cell death.

00:28:19.070 --> 00:28:21.340
These cell deaths occur
at different points

00:28:21.340 --> 00:28:21.970
in the lineage.

00:28:21.970 --> 00:28:23.886
Here we look at the
development of the feeding

00:28:23.886 --> 00:28:26.440
organ, the pharynx,
and basically you

00:28:26.440 --> 00:28:29.410
find that the cell deaths
occur at different times,

00:28:29.410 --> 00:28:32.800
different points in the lineage,
and with nearest relatives

00:28:32.800 --> 00:28:37.390
varying quite substantially
over the sister cells.

00:28:37.390 --> 00:28:41.520
When you look at these
deaths, what you see,

00:28:41.520 --> 00:28:45.270
again using no Nomarski
optics, is most obvious here,

00:28:45.270 --> 00:28:47.070
this flat disk
like appearance is

00:28:47.070 --> 00:28:51.630
a cell in the throes of death,
and later that cell goes away,

00:28:51.630 --> 00:28:54.670
no sign of it can be seen.

00:28:54.670 --> 00:28:58.360
Looking at the deaths with
an electron microscope,

00:28:58.360 --> 00:29:01.390
they have specific ultra
structural features, most

00:29:01.390 --> 00:29:05.600
obvious here is the electron
density of this dying cell.

00:29:05.600 --> 00:29:07.480
There are other
features as well,

00:29:07.480 --> 00:29:11.560
which told us that these deaths
looked like a process that

00:29:11.560 --> 00:29:15.010
had been defined in
pathological specimens

00:29:15.010 --> 00:29:20.740
from human patients of
cells that were dying

00:29:20.740 --> 00:29:25.360
and were called, the process
was called apoptosis.

00:29:25.360 --> 00:29:27.910
So programmed cell
death in c. elegans

00:29:27.910 --> 00:29:30.730
is similar to mammalian
apoptosis, which I should add

00:29:30.730 --> 00:29:35.120
is pronounced in any
of a variety of ways.

00:29:35.120 --> 00:29:40.610
So what we decided, then,
was that since cells

00:29:40.610 --> 00:29:45.170
die in a specific
morphological, through

00:29:45.170 --> 00:29:49.100
a specific morphological set
of events, at different times,

00:29:49.100 --> 00:29:51.140
in different places
in the lineage,

00:29:51.140 --> 00:29:53.540
that we could think about
programmed cell death as a cell

00:29:53.540 --> 00:29:53.870
fate.

00:29:53.870 --> 00:29:55.370
No different from
any other fate,

00:29:55.370 --> 00:29:58.730
like differentiating into a gut
cell, or a serotonergic nerve

00:29:58.730 --> 00:30:00.710
cell, or whatever
it happens to be.

00:30:00.710 --> 00:30:03.500
And if that's true,
then we postulated

00:30:03.500 --> 00:30:06.650
there ought to be genes that
control that fate, that control

00:30:06.650 --> 00:30:09.140
the decision should
cells live or die,

00:30:09.140 --> 00:30:11.150
and that having made
the decision to die,

00:30:11.150 --> 00:30:14.000
should carry out that
process of death.

00:30:14.000 --> 00:30:17.180
And so what we did was
look for, identify,

00:30:17.180 --> 00:30:19.550
and characterize such genes.

00:30:19.550 --> 00:30:22.340
And those studies
led to the definition

00:30:22.340 --> 00:30:26.580
of a genetic pathway,
as outlined here.

00:30:26.580 --> 00:30:29.850
This pathway consists of
essentially four sequential

00:30:29.850 --> 00:30:31.270
steps.

00:30:31.270 --> 00:30:33.870
The first step is
a decision step.

00:30:33.870 --> 00:30:36.660
Every cell in the body
has to decide, I will live

00:30:36.660 --> 00:30:41.000
or I will die by killing myself.

00:30:41.000 --> 00:30:47.070
The second step is the
killing step itself.

00:30:47.070 --> 00:30:50.690
That's what
generates the corpse.

00:30:50.690 --> 00:30:54.770
The third step, basically,
is getting rid of the body.

00:30:54.770 --> 00:30:57.830
It's a process of engulfment
whereby the dead cell

00:30:57.830 --> 00:30:59.840
is removed from the animal.

00:30:59.840 --> 00:31:03.230
And the fourth step is
eliminating the debris

00:31:03.230 --> 00:31:07.060
of that cellular corpse.

00:31:07.060 --> 00:31:11.680
Let me briefly tell
you about these steps.

00:31:11.680 --> 00:31:16.590
Some years ago Hilary
Ellis, shown here,

00:31:16.590 --> 00:31:21.400
and Chan Desai, shown here,
both as graduate students

00:31:21.400 --> 00:31:26.290
in the lab, identified two genes
with functions needed for cells

00:31:26.290 --> 00:31:28.615
to die by programmed cell death.

00:31:28.615 --> 00:31:33.310
These genes are called CED-3
CED for cell death, abnormal.

00:31:33.310 --> 00:31:35.182
CED-3 and CED-4.

00:31:35.182 --> 00:31:38.950
And if either of these
genes is inactivated,

00:31:38.950 --> 00:31:43.090
essentially all of the cells
that should die instead live.

00:31:43.090 --> 00:31:48.760
And it was this finding that
said that programmed cell death

00:31:48.760 --> 00:31:53.920
is an active, biologic process.

00:31:53.920 --> 00:31:58.210
Then Junying Yuan, as
a graduate student,

00:31:58.210 --> 00:32:01.870
performed so-called
genetic mosaic analyzes,

00:32:01.870 --> 00:32:05.560
asking the question in what
cells must these genes act

00:32:05.560 --> 00:32:07.810
in order for death to occur?

00:32:07.810 --> 00:32:11.990
And the answer was in those
cells that are going to die.

00:32:11.990 --> 00:32:14.330
Conclusion,
programmed cell death

00:32:14.330 --> 00:32:20.150
is, at least to this extent,
a process of cellular suicide.

00:32:20.150 --> 00:32:24.690
Junying and another graduate
student, Shai Shaham,

00:32:24.690 --> 00:32:28.440
then worked together to clone
and molecularly characterize

00:32:28.440 --> 00:32:30.180
these genes.

00:32:30.180 --> 00:32:35.840
And what they found is that
first these two killer genes,

00:32:35.840 --> 00:32:38.620
CED-3 and CED-4,
first CED-3, looks

00:32:38.620 --> 00:32:43.420
similar to a human protein
known as ICE, interleukin 1 beta

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:46.240
converting enzyme, a protein
that had been purified

00:32:46.240 --> 00:32:49.240
by two pharmaceutical
companies interested

00:32:49.240 --> 00:32:52.120
in human inflammatory disease.

00:32:52.120 --> 00:32:55.960
And this information told
us something about CED-3,

00:32:55.960 --> 00:32:59.440
because they had
shown that ICE acted

00:32:59.440 --> 00:33:03.280
as a protease, an enzyme
that cleaves other proteins,

00:33:03.280 --> 00:33:07.990
suggesting to us that CED-3
kills by acting as a protease.

00:33:07.990 --> 00:33:10.690
And [? Ding Sho ?] in
the lab confirmed that.

00:33:10.690 --> 00:33:15.220
And also suggesting, that since
there was a human counterpart,

00:33:15.220 --> 00:33:18.520
that ICE or other
CED-3 ICE-like proteins

00:33:18.520 --> 00:33:21.760
may well play a comparable
role in the process

00:33:21.760 --> 00:33:25.390
of programmed cell death
apoptosis in humans.

00:33:25.390 --> 00:33:29.080
And there has now been much
evidence supporting that.

00:33:29.080 --> 00:33:33.400
The second gene, CED-4, also
has a human counterpart,

00:33:33.400 --> 00:33:36.340
identified through biochemical
studies done by [? Shel Don ?]

00:33:36.340 --> 00:33:38.370
[? Wong ?] in Dallas, Texas.

00:33:38.370 --> 00:33:41.230
Biochemical studies in which
he was assaying in a test

00:33:41.230 --> 00:33:44.230
tube the process of
programmed cell death,

00:33:44.230 --> 00:33:47.840
and found an activity that
activated this process.

00:33:47.840 --> 00:33:50.470
And it turned out
to look like CED-4,

00:33:50.470 --> 00:33:54.750
which we had already described.

00:33:54.750 --> 00:33:59.160
The third c. elegans
gene that plays

00:33:59.160 --> 00:34:04.170
a key role in this core killing
part of programmed cell death

00:34:04.170 --> 00:34:05.520
is known as CED-9.

00:34:05.520 --> 00:34:08.280
And CED-9 is different.

00:34:08.280 --> 00:34:11.250
Because whereas CED-3
and CED-4 are killers,

00:34:11.250 --> 00:34:13.350
CED-9 is a protector.

00:34:13.350 --> 00:34:15.810
It keeps cells alive.

00:34:15.810 --> 00:34:20.500
CED-9 was discovered by
graduate student Ron Ellis,

00:34:20.500 --> 00:34:24.699
and characterized molecularly
by graduate student Michael

00:34:24.699 --> 00:34:26.500
Hengartner.

00:34:26.500 --> 00:34:29.920
And what Michael found
was that CED-9 also

00:34:29.920 --> 00:34:34.600
looked like a human protein,
this one that had been isolated

00:34:34.600 --> 00:34:38.710
because of its involvement
in cancer, a protein known

00:34:38.710 --> 00:34:43.040
as BCL-2, were BCL stands
for b-cell lymphoma.

00:34:43.040 --> 00:34:48.219
BCL-2 is proto-oncogene,
causally responsible

00:34:48.219 --> 00:34:49.530
for follicular lymphoma.

00:34:49.530 --> 00:34:52.540
It's what I referred to earlier.

00:34:52.540 --> 00:34:57.910
OK, so we have CED-9,
CED-4, and CED-3.

00:34:57.910 --> 00:35:01.870
And Shai Shaham analyzed through
genetics the interactions

00:35:01.870 --> 00:35:06.100
amongst these genes, and
defined the core genetic pathway

00:35:06.100 --> 00:35:10.120
for programmed cell death,
as shown here, where it CED-3

00:35:10.120 --> 00:35:14.620
kills, CED-4 kills by
causing CED-3 to kill,

00:35:14.620 --> 00:35:17.050
and CED-9 protects,
by preventing CED-4

00:35:17.050 --> 00:35:20.740
from killing, causing
CED-3 to kill.

00:35:20.740 --> 00:35:22.576
Given the mammalian
counterparts,

00:35:22.576 --> 00:35:27.670
we postulated a
pathway as shown here.

00:35:27.670 --> 00:35:32.650
With BCL-2, the Apaf-1
CED-4-like factor,

00:35:32.650 --> 00:35:37.820
and these proteases, that
are today known as caspases.

00:35:37.820 --> 00:35:41.190
So this is the key core pathway.

00:35:43.950 --> 00:35:44.950
What else?

00:35:48.450 --> 00:35:51.690
Barbara Conrad, a
post-doc in the lab,

00:35:51.690 --> 00:35:55.230
analyzed another gene, egl-1
a gene that we had actually

00:35:55.230 --> 00:36:01.560
studied and discovered in the
course of studies of behavior.

00:36:01.560 --> 00:36:03.680
But it turned out the
behavioral abnormality

00:36:03.680 --> 00:36:08.450
was reflecting abnormalities
in programmed cell death.

00:36:08.450 --> 00:36:11.291
And what Barbara
discovered was that egl-1

00:36:11.291 --> 00:36:15.660
just like CED-3 and
CED-4 is a killer.

00:36:15.660 --> 00:36:19.982
We won't go through data but
you can see egl-1, like CED-3

00:36:19.982 --> 00:36:22.550
and CED-4 here.

00:36:22.550 --> 00:36:24.740
And the genetic
studies she did showed

00:36:24.740 --> 00:36:27.872
that although it was a killer,
it was different from CED-3

00:36:27.872 --> 00:36:31.790
and CED-4, because
instead of acting,

00:36:31.790 --> 00:36:34.670
were CED-3 and CED-4 act
downstream of the protector

00:36:34.670 --> 00:36:39.670
CED-9, it acts
upstream of CED-9.

00:36:39.670 --> 00:36:44.170
She cloned and characterized
this gene and, whoops.

00:36:44.170 --> 00:36:46.210
First pathway.

00:36:46.210 --> 00:36:48.520
Pathway, egl-1 upstream.

00:36:48.520 --> 00:36:52.510
So egl-1 kills by preventing
CED-9 from preventing CED-4

00:36:52.510 --> 00:36:55.280
from activating CED-3.

00:36:55.280 --> 00:36:55.990
Right?

00:36:55.990 --> 00:36:57.620
Pathway.

00:36:57.620 --> 00:36:58.670
What is it?

00:36:58.670 --> 00:37:00.140
Barbara cloned it.

00:37:00.140 --> 00:37:03.710
And it turned out to
encode a gene related

00:37:03.710 --> 00:37:08.140
to BCL-2, the Proto-oncogene.

00:37:08.140 --> 00:37:12.880
And it turns out that there is
a family of these BCL-2-like

00:37:12.880 --> 00:37:14.580
genes in us.

00:37:14.580 --> 00:37:17.260
And egl-1 looks like
another one of these.

00:37:17.260 --> 00:37:20.140
Some of these genes,
like the BCL-2

00:37:20.140 --> 00:37:21.580
protect against cell death.

00:37:21.580 --> 00:37:27.030
Others, like egl-1,
cause cell death.

00:37:27.030 --> 00:37:34.620
So egl-1 is upstream, now,
of CED-9 in this pathway.

00:37:34.620 --> 00:37:37.490
And it was known at that
point that BCL-2 members

00:37:37.490 --> 00:37:40.070
could physically
interact with each other.

00:37:40.070 --> 00:37:44.780
So Barbara asked the question
what about CED-9 and egl-1,

00:37:44.780 --> 00:37:48.440
and she found that they
interact physically.

00:37:48.440 --> 00:37:50.090
And Furthermore,
in studies we did,

00:37:50.090 --> 00:37:52.850
and that were also done in
many other laboratories,

00:37:52.850 --> 00:37:56.600
it became clear that
said CED-9 and CED-4 also

00:37:56.600 --> 00:38:02.390
interact physically, leading
us now to a molecular pathway,

00:38:02.390 --> 00:38:06.230
as indicated here,
where we say egl-1

00:38:06.230 --> 00:38:08.600
is a direct negative
regulator of CED-9.

00:38:08.600 --> 00:38:12.020
So egl-1 prevents CED-9 nine
from acting by physically

00:38:12.020 --> 00:38:13.460
interacting with it.

00:38:13.460 --> 00:38:18.290
And then CED-9 prevents CED-4
from acting by physically

00:38:18.290 --> 00:38:20.030
interacting with it.

00:38:20.030 --> 00:38:24.540
And that's what the genetic
and molecular studies indicate.

00:38:24.540 --> 00:38:27.070
What is it well one does?

00:38:27.070 --> 00:38:30.460
We think the answer to that
is it changes the location

00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:33.420
in the cell of CED-4.

00:38:33.420 --> 00:38:35.830
And the way in which
we discovered that

00:38:35.830 --> 00:38:38.350
was through a series of
experiments done in part

00:38:38.350 --> 00:38:40.420
by Barbara Conrad,
whom I've already

00:38:40.420 --> 00:38:44.590
shown you a picture of,
and also by Brad Hirsch,

00:38:44.590 --> 00:38:47.230
the graduate student
in the lab, Zheng

00:38:47.230 --> 00:38:52.750
Zhou, a post-doc in the
lab, and Fangli Chen, who

00:38:52.750 --> 00:38:55.180
was an undergraduate
student in the lab.

00:38:55.180 --> 00:38:57.550
And what they did
was to use antibodies

00:38:57.550 --> 00:39:00.850
against these various proteins
involved in cell death,

00:39:00.850 --> 00:39:03.790
and ask the question,
where are the proteins?

00:39:03.790 --> 00:39:06.850
And what they found
first was that CED-9

00:39:06.850 --> 00:39:09.810
is localized to mitochondria.

00:39:09.810 --> 00:39:12.540
OK, what we're looking at here
is a developing c. elegans

00:39:12.540 --> 00:39:13.720
embryo.

00:39:13.720 --> 00:39:17.080
This panel shows where
mitochondria are.

00:39:17.080 --> 00:39:20.260
And basically, CED-9
and mitochondria

00:39:20.260 --> 00:39:22.700
are in the same location.

00:39:22.700 --> 00:39:25.060
So CED-9 is in mitochondria.

00:39:25.060 --> 00:39:30.400
They further showed that
CED-4 is also in mitochondria.

00:39:30.400 --> 00:39:32.200
And remember, I've
told you CED-9

00:39:32.200 --> 00:39:34.910
and CED-4 physically interact.

00:39:34.910 --> 00:39:38.230
So the question,
then, was is either

00:39:38.230 --> 00:39:42.730
required for the localization
to mitochondria of the other?

00:39:42.730 --> 00:39:44.530
And that we could
ask very simply,

00:39:44.530 --> 00:39:48.550
because we had mutants that were
lacking, either CED-4 CED-9.

00:39:48.550 --> 00:39:51.460
So in the absence of CED-9,
was CED-4 still mitochondrial?

00:39:51.460 --> 00:39:55.570
In the absence of CED-4, was
CED-9 nine still mitochondrial?

00:39:55.570 --> 00:39:58.540
Again, without going
through details,

00:39:58.540 --> 00:40:03.730
the answer was,
CED-4 localization

00:40:03.730 --> 00:40:06.220
depends upon CED-9.

00:40:06.220 --> 00:40:09.610
And in the absence of CED-9,
you see a very different

00:40:09.610 --> 00:40:10.810
localization pattern.

00:40:10.810 --> 00:40:15.520
Here is CED-4 normally,
here is CED-4 without CED-9.

00:40:15.520 --> 00:40:17.770
And where it is, is
instead of mitochondria,

00:40:17.770 --> 00:40:23.880
it's located around the
nucleus, in a perinuclear way.

00:40:23.880 --> 00:40:26.910
A series of studies
that went from here

00:40:26.910 --> 00:40:30.180
led us to the model
that's shown here.

00:40:30.180 --> 00:40:34.380
And that is that this egl-1
protein, this killer protein,

00:40:34.380 --> 00:40:40.880
what it does, is it interacts
with CED-9 on the mitochondria,

00:40:40.880 --> 00:40:44.780
displacing, or at least
causing the release of CED-4,

00:40:44.780 --> 00:40:48.140
and the translocation
of CED-4 to the nucleus,

00:40:48.140 --> 00:40:51.950
allowing CED-4 to be active,
and thereby activate CED-3

00:40:51.950 --> 00:40:57.488
and cause programmed cell death.

00:40:57.488 --> 00:40:58.360
OK?

00:40:58.360 --> 00:41:00.660
So that's the basic model.

00:41:00.660 --> 00:41:05.190
That is our core pathway
for programmed cell death.

00:41:05.190 --> 00:41:08.220
What I want to do now,
is to turn quickly

00:41:08.220 --> 00:41:10.390
to the next step
in this pathway,

00:41:10.390 --> 00:41:12.840
the step of engulfment.

00:41:12.840 --> 00:41:14.990
When a cell dies by
programmed cell death,

00:41:14.990 --> 00:41:20.070
it is swallowed and
destroyed by another cell.

00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:23.600
We have analyzed that
process in much the same way

00:41:23.600 --> 00:41:26.520
as we've analyzed
the killing process.

00:41:26.520 --> 00:41:32.400
I won't tell you how we got
there, but here's where we got.

00:41:32.400 --> 00:41:35.250
There are two
parallel and partially

00:41:35.250 --> 00:41:41.050
redundant pathways that lead to
the engulfment of cell corpses.

00:41:41.050 --> 00:41:45.460
There are three genes that act
in the order CED-716 in one

00:41:45.460 --> 00:41:48.760
pathway, and four genes
that have been identified

00:41:48.760 --> 00:41:50.970
that act in the order
2, 5, 12 together,

00:41:50.970 --> 00:41:53.930
and 10 in the second pathway.

00:41:53.930 --> 00:41:56.450
These two pathways
appear to be involved

00:41:56.450 --> 00:42:02.630
in the communication between
the dying cell and the cell that

00:42:02.630 --> 00:42:03.800
is swallowing it.

00:42:03.800 --> 00:42:06.780
Signal transduction pathways.

00:42:06.780 --> 00:42:09.510
In one case, what we
believe is going on

00:42:09.510 --> 00:42:12.540
is that the dying
cell uses CED-7,

00:42:12.540 --> 00:42:14.400
which encodes of protein
of a family known

00:42:14.400 --> 00:42:18.360
as ABC transporters, to
basically put on its surface

00:42:18.360 --> 00:42:20.580
a signal that says
come and get me.

00:42:23.200 --> 00:42:26.890
The engulfing cell
has a receptor

00:42:26.890 --> 00:42:30.370
that recognizes that
signal, and then transmits

00:42:30.370 --> 00:42:34.220
that signal via another
protein and a whole pathway,

00:42:34.220 --> 00:42:36.710
which has yet to be determined.

00:42:36.710 --> 00:42:39.110
And parallel to this
process, there's

00:42:39.110 --> 00:42:42.550
another process of
intercellular communication.

00:42:42.550 --> 00:42:45.620
Again, much of it
is not yet known.

00:42:45.620 --> 00:42:49.190
We think the corpse talks
to the engulfing cell,

00:42:49.190 --> 00:42:52.880
and signals, now via
a protein complex,

00:42:52.880 --> 00:42:57.140
that is responsible for
activating a protein that

00:42:57.140 --> 00:43:02.480
is known to be involved in
cytoskeletal re-organization.

00:43:02.480 --> 00:43:04.310
That means changes
in cell shape.

00:43:04.310 --> 00:43:08.150
When one cell swallows
another, it changes its shape,

00:43:08.150 --> 00:43:11.030
and this is how we
think it does it.

00:43:11.030 --> 00:43:13.190
OK, now I'm not going to
talk about the work that

00:43:13.190 --> 00:43:15.600
led to this model, or the
details of this model,

00:43:15.600 --> 00:43:19.580
but rather use it to
introduce another question.

00:43:19.580 --> 00:43:21.950
So I've been talking
about programmed cell

00:43:21.950 --> 00:43:24.540
death as cellular suicide.

00:43:24.540 --> 00:43:28.350
But we've been suspicious for
some time about this process

00:43:28.350 --> 00:43:29.730
of engulfment.

00:43:29.730 --> 00:43:32.490
If you think about it,
if you were on the scene

00:43:32.490 --> 00:43:38.100
and you watched something die,
and as that death was occurring

00:43:38.100 --> 00:43:43.960
somebody else was right there
surrounding that individual,

00:43:43.960 --> 00:43:45.210
you might be suspicious.

00:43:45.210 --> 00:43:47.790
Now, in fact programmed
cell deaths in c. elegans,

00:43:47.790 --> 00:43:50.070
the engulfment
process starts even

00:43:50.070 --> 00:43:52.530
before the cell division that
generates the cell that's

00:43:52.530 --> 00:43:54.490
going to die is completed.

00:43:54.490 --> 00:43:56.310
So we had been
suspicious for some time

00:43:56.310 --> 00:44:00.560
that maybe cell death
isn't entirely suicide.

00:44:00.560 --> 00:44:02.900
Maybe there's a
component of murder.

00:44:06.110 --> 00:44:11.030
But we couldn't get enough
evidence to convict.

00:44:11.030 --> 00:44:15.020
Peter Reddien, a graduate
student in the lab,

00:44:15.020 --> 00:44:17.180
obtained the evidence.

00:44:17.180 --> 00:44:21.440
So he started with the
background knowledge that

00:44:21.440 --> 00:44:24.050
is a mutant defective
and engulfment,

00:44:24.050 --> 00:44:26.340
programmed cell
death still occurs.

00:44:26.340 --> 00:44:28.400
So the first order
statement is OK,

00:44:28.400 --> 00:44:31.429
engulfment's not needed
for programmed cell death.

00:44:31.429 --> 00:44:33.470
But Peter said no, I want
to go a little further.

00:44:33.470 --> 00:44:37.260
I want to really address
this in a more rigorous way.

00:44:37.260 --> 00:44:38.900
And what he did, was
he took advantage

00:44:38.900 --> 00:44:40.970
of an assay that had
been developed in the lab

00:44:40.970 --> 00:44:43.460
by postdoc Scott Cameron.

00:44:43.460 --> 00:44:45.200
And this was an
assay that allowed

00:44:45.200 --> 00:44:48.830
us to look very
easily for cells that

00:44:48.830 --> 00:44:51.260
should die, but instead live.

00:44:51.260 --> 00:44:53.450
And if you think about
it, that's not always

00:44:53.450 --> 00:44:54.470
the easiest thing to do.

00:44:54.470 --> 00:44:56.480
Because you've got an
animal with an awful lot,

00:44:56.480 --> 00:44:58.700
959 doesn't sound like
a lot, but if you're

00:44:58.700 --> 00:45:00.980
going to look for
one extra, you better

00:45:00.980 --> 00:45:03.950
know how to do that
in a very precise way.

00:45:03.950 --> 00:45:06.170
And Scott developed
a way to do this.

00:45:06.170 --> 00:45:08.720
What he did was, he took
advantage of a gene called

00:45:08.720 --> 00:45:11.780
lin-11, characterized
by Gwen Actin when

00:45:11.780 --> 00:45:15.320
she was a graduate student in
the lab, and which she showed

00:45:15.320 --> 00:45:20.210
was expressed in a set of cells
derived from those p cells.

00:45:20.210 --> 00:45:23.270
Remember, I showed you those
p cell divisions and p cell

00:45:23.270 --> 00:45:24.822
lineage right in the beginning?

00:45:24.822 --> 00:45:26.280
Well there aren't
just two of them,

00:45:26.280 --> 00:45:27.890
there are actually
more than that.

00:45:27.890 --> 00:45:31.160
There are 12 p cells
and a friend called w.

00:45:31.160 --> 00:45:35.210
And these cells all divide
to generate six descendants,

00:45:35.210 --> 00:45:37.640
except for w, which
does only five.

00:45:37.640 --> 00:45:40.220
And the corresponding
descendants

00:45:40.220 --> 00:45:43.910
of each of these lineages
differentiate in the same way,

00:45:43.910 --> 00:45:45.590
in general, most of
them becoming nerve

00:45:45.590 --> 00:45:48.872
cells for particular types.

00:45:48.872 --> 00:45:50.900
But, there's a difference.

00:45:50.900 --> 00:45:53.840
Six of these cells derive
from p-3 through 8,

00:45:53.840 --> 00:45:58.100
survive to become motor
neurons known as vc neurons.

00:45:58.100 --> 00:46:00.740
But their counterparts from
the other cell lineages

00:46:00.740 --> 00:46:02.750
in the front and
back of the animal

00:46:02.750 --> 00:46:05.120
undergo programmed cell death.

00:46:05.120 --> 00:46:08.210
Now, if we block programmed
cell death, for example,

00:46:08.210 --> 00:46:11.720
with a mutation in
the CED-3 killer gene,

00:46:11.720 --> 00:46:13.550
then all of these
cells survive, and they

00:46:13.550 --> 00:46:17.580
take on the characteristics
of these nerve cells.

00:46:17.580 --> 00:46:20.630
So, we then could
use a reporter,

00:46:20.630 --> 00:46:24.110
expressed in these nerve
cells, and basically

00:46:24.110 --> 00:46:26.030
use a reporter
that's fluorescent,

00:46:26.030 --> 00:46:29.360
that uses the green fluorescent
protein, characterized by Marty

00:46:29.360 --> 00:46:31.670
Chalfie at Columbia University.

00:46:31.670 --> 00:46:33.957
And ask if we ever
see fluorescent cells

00:46:33.957 --> 00:46:36.290
in the front or back of the
animal, where normally there

00:46:36.290 --> 00:46:40.110
would be none, in a mutant
defective in engulfment.

00:46:40.110 --> 00:46:43.820
And I wouldn't tell you this
if the answer weren't yes.

00:46:43.820 --> 00:46:45.920
So here we see a
wild type animal.

00:46:45.920 --> 00:46:48.110
And you see green spots.

00:46:48.110 --> 00:46:51.520
You also see expression
in another cell type here.

00:46:51.520 --> 00:46:55.150
And a CED-3 mutant, no cell
death, lots of green spots.

00:46:55.150 --> 00:46:58.840
And here's a CED-7 mutant,
defective in that signal

00:46:58.840 --> 00:46:59.970
on the corpse.

00:46:59.970 --> 00:47:01.467
Says come and get me.

00:47:01.467 --> 00:47:02.300
And what do you see?

00:47:02.300 --> 00:47:05.740
You see a cell up here,
where in the normal animal

00:47:05.740 --> 00:47:07.600
there is never one.

00:47:07.600 --> 00:47:11.200
So you block engulfment,
and you get some survival.

00:47:11.200 --> 00:47:14.620
Peter quantified it,
showed it was real,

00:47:14.620 --> 00:47:18.340
and showed that it was true for
all of the engulfment genes,

00:47:18.340 --> 00:47:22.960
leading to the conclusion
that the engulfment process is

00:47:22.960 --> 00:47:29.666
necessary for total
cell death to occur.

00:47:35.390 --> 00:47:38.300
Now Peter also used
no Nomarski optics

00:47:38.300 --> 00:47:41.780
to watch this process
in living worms.

00:47:41.780 --> 00:47:44.300
And what he found was
something quite striking.

00:47:44.300 --> 00:47:47.450
So here is a cartoon version
of what I showed you already

00:47:47.450 --> 00:47:49.730
as a photograph,
series of photographs,

00:47:49.730 --> 00:47:51.710
process of programmed
cell death.

00:47:51.710 --> 00:47:54.110
In a CED-3 mutant this
doesn't occur at all.

00:47:54.110 --> 00:47:56.420
In a mutant defective in
engulfment what happens is

00:47:56.420 --> 00:47:57.650
this process begins.

00:47:57.650 --> 00:48:00.860
There's no engulfing cell
that's active and swallowing it.

00:48:00.860 --> 00:48:04.340
The process begins, but
then it can regress.

00:48:04.340 --> 00:48:06.330
And in fact, it can be episodic.

00:48:06.330 --> 00:48:08.840
So that a cell starts to die,
comes back, looks normal,

00:48:08.840 --> 00:48:11.270
starts to die again,
comes back, looks normal.

00:48:11.270 --> 00:48:14.360
Back and forth,
and generally dies.

00:48:14.360 --> 00:48:16.900
But occasionally doesn't.

00:48:16.900 --> 00:48:19.810
In the absence of
engulfment, it looks

00:48:19.810 --> 00:48:22.160
like cells are kind of
poised on a knife edge,

00:48:22.160 --> 00:48:27.280
biased towards death,
usually die, but not always.

00:48:27.280 --> 00:48:32.230
Now I think this has
implications for biology,

00:48:32.230 --> 00:48:34.480
and also possibly for therapy.

00:48:34.480 --> 00:48:36.400
Implicit in what
I've told you already

00:48:36.400 --> 00:48:39.140
about the involvement of
programmed cell death,

00:48:39.140 --> 00:48:42.280
in a variety of
diseases, is the fact

00:48:42.280 --> 00:48:45.760
that these genes define
potential targets

00:48:45.760 --> 00:48:47.890
for intervention.

00:48:47.890 --> 00:48:50.939
If you block the process of
cell death, in some disorders

00:48:50.939 --> 00:48:51.730
that would be good.

00:48:51.730 --> 00:48:54.940
If you cause the process of
cell death, in some disorders

00:48:54.940 --> 00:48:56.630
that would be good too.

00:48:56.630 --> 00:48:58.840
Now, obviously we
have to be careful.

00:48:58.840 --> 00:49:03.400
And as with all biology, genes
and targets aren't enough.

00:49:03.400 --> 00:49:07.150
To know either the biology
or the therapeutics,

00:49:07.150 --> 00:49:10.260
you have to really do
the biological studies.

00:49:10.260 --> 00:49:12.610
But there are ways
of pursuing that.

00:49:12.610 --> 00:49:14.580
And that, in fact,
is being done.

00:49:14.580 --> 00:49:17.280
From these studies I
want to add the comment

00:49:17.280 --> 00:49:20.130
that maybe the engulfment
process involves

00:49:20.130 --> 00:49:23.340
another biologic phenomena
and another set of targets.

00:49:23.340 --> 00:49:25.440
Because in many of
these disorders,

00:49:25.440 --> 00:49:27.300
it's likely that
in fact cells are

00:49:27.300 --> 00:49:29.340
poised on such a knife edge.

00:49:29.340 --> 00:49:35.730
And maybe we can push
them one way or the other.

00:49:35.730 --> 00:49:37.870
OK, now, having
said that, I'd like

00:49:37.870 --> 00:49:44.170
to finish basically by telling
you a little bit of some

00:49:44.170 --> 00:49:45.910
of the newest.

00:49:45.910 --> 00:49:50.050
And that is that Peter used
this assay that I just told you

00:49:50.050 --> 00:49:53.520
about here, oh and I should
say, this was the conclusion,

00:49:53.520 --> 00:49:55.060
engulfment is responsible.

00:49:55.060 --> 00:49:58.120
He used this assay, now
very sensitive assay,

00:49:58.120 --> 00:50:00.790
looking for extra
green fluorescent spots

00:50:00.790 --> 00:50:03.820
to try to find new
genes that might have

00:50:03.820 --> 00:50:06.670
a partial role, like
the in engulfment genes,

00:50:06.670 --> 00:50:08.980
in the cell killing process.

00:50:08.980 --> 00:50:12.610
And what he found,
again, by using

00:50:12.610 --> 00:50:15.880
this green fluorescent
protein assay, and looking

00:50:15.880 --> 00:50:19.570
for mutations that would tweak
the number of living or dying

00:50:19.570 --> 00:50:21.800
cells, what he
found, for example,

00:50:21.800 --> 00:50:24.460
in a so-called enhancer
screen, looking

00:50:24.460 --> 00:50:28.660
to make the effects of a week
CED-3 mutations stronger,

00:50:28.660 --> 00:50:31.720
making a partial killer
into a stronger killer,

00:50:31.720 --> 00:50:35.980
he found mutations,
which we call n3376 3380,

00:50:35.980 --> 00:50:41.140
the 3376th and 80th mutation
characterized in the lab.

00:50:41.140 --> 00:50:45.610
If these two mutations
define two genes involved

00:50:45.610 --> 00:50:48.370
in the killing process
that we didn't know

00:50:48.370 --> 00:50:51.160
were involved in
the killing process.

00:50:51.160 --> 00:50:56.090
The first of them
encodes a novel protein

00:50:56.090 --> 00:51:00.380
that has a motif that is
suggestive of interactions

00:51:00.380 --> 00:51:03.050
with other macromolecules, and
as I'll tell you in a moment,

00:51:03.050 --> 00:51:05.840
we think it's actually
interaction with DNA.

00:51:05.840 --> 00:51:09.620
The gene is called mcd-1,
for modifier of cell death.

00:51:09.620 --> 00:51:15.140
And the second gene, much to
our surprise, was an old friend.

00:51:15.140 --> 00:51:16.880
The second gene,
the second mutation,

00:51:16.880 --> 00:51:23.910
turned out to be an allele of
a gene that we called dpl-1.

00:51:23.910 --> 00:51:26.850
From studies we had
done in what I thought

00:51:26.850 --> 00:51:31.290
was a totally unrelated project
of an oncogene pathway involved

00:51:31.290 --> 00:51:34.980
in cell signaling in
c. elegans development.

00:51:34.980 --> 00:51:37.860
Oncogene, the RAS oncogene.

00:51:37.860 --> 00:51:43.400
An oncogene discovered here by
Bob Weinberg some years ago.

00:51:43.400 --> 00:51:45.870
Now, dp proteins are
known in mammals,

00:51:45.870 --> 00:51:47.850
they affect cell
proliferation, and they also

00:51:47.850 --> 00:51:50.670
affect cell survival,
and cell death.

00:51:50.670 --> 00:51:53.410
But nobody knows how.

00:51:53.410 --> 00:51:59.150
They act by interacting with
other proteins, like e2-f.

00:51:59.150 --> 00:52:02.950
And they interact with Rb
proteins, retinoblastoma,

00:52:02.950 --> 00:52:05.270
another gene that's been
characterized by Bob Weinberg

00:52:05.270 --> 00:52:10.650
and by Tyler
Jacques here at MIT.

00:52:10.650 --> 00:52:14.990
So, we had already analyzed
the roles of dp protein,

00:52:14.990 --> 00:52:17.690
and e2-f protein, and
an Rb protein in the c.

00:52:17.690 --> 00:52:18.950
elegans RAS pathway.

00:52:18.950 --> 00:52:20.930
We had all the reagents.

00:52:20.930 --> 00:52:23.930
And Peter said, well,
are they also involved

00:52:23.930 --> 00:52:26.160
in this process of cell death?

00:52:26.160 --> 00:52:28.430
And again, don't worry
about the details,

00:52:28.430 --> 00:52:30.110
just worry about the title.

00:52:30.110 --> 00:52:35.540
The answer is yes, dp,
e-2f, and Rb in the worm

00:52:35.540 --> 00:52:41.330
are all involved in
programmed cell death.

00:52:41.330 --> 00:52:42.980
What they do, we don't know.

00:52:42.980 --> 00:52:44.990
But given what we
do know about what

00:52:44.990 --> 00:52:48.140
they do in other contexts,
what we postulate

00:52:48.140 --> 00:52:51.380
is they act by
controlling transcription,

00:52:51.380 --> 00:52:54.960
RNA synthesis, gene expression.

00:52:54.960 --> 00:53:00.170
We know that they act downstream
of the protector gene CED-9.

00:53:00.170 --> 00:53:02.180
And we have two
basic hypotheses:

00:53:02.180 --> 00:53:05.090
either that they are regulating
the expression of the killer

00:53:05.090 --> 00:53:09.110
gene CED-3 and/or CED-4,
or that they interface

00:53:09.110 --> 00:53:11.270
in this pathway in
a different way,

00:53:11.270 --> 00:53:14.120
possibly with genes involved
in the cell cycle, something we

00:53:14.120 --> 00:53:16.890
know that these genes can do.

00:53:16.890 --> 00:53:22.490
And that's something that
we are currently exploring.

00:53:22.490 --> 00:53:26.010
Now, the last step in this
pathway, you'll recall,

00:53:26.010 --> 00:53:30.770
is that of degrading
the cellular corpse.

00:53:30.770 --> 00:53:34.190
And there's one gene in this
step that was identified

00:53:34.190 --> 00:53:36.680
many years ago by John Sulston.

00:53:36.680 --> 00:53:41.390
It's a gene he called
nuc-1, possibly a bad name,

00:53:41.390 --> 00:53:44.180
because he showed that it
controlled the nuclease.

00:53:44.180 --> 00:53:46.460
And what happens is
that this nuclease

00:53:46.460 --> 00:53:49.720
is involved in degrading
the DNA in cell corpses.

00:53:49.720 --> 00:53:52.430
And if it's not active,
the DNA remains.

00:53:52.430 --> 00:53:54.500
But other aspects
of the death process

00:53:54.500 --> 00:53:58.670
occur, and one is left with
these little kinetic nuclei,

00:53:58.670 --> 00:54:00.830
as shown here.

00:54:00.830 --> 00:54:03.460
And that's how John
found this nuc.

00:54:03.460 --> 00:54:06.520
He showed that it controlled
the activity of a nuclease,

00:54:06.520 --> 00:54:10.170
but it wasn't clear whether this
control was direct or indirect.

00:54:10.170 --> 00:54:14.550
And it also wasn't obviously at
all clear what kind of nuclease

00:54:14.550 --> 00:54:15.220
it might be.

00:54:15.220 --> 00:54:17.430
And if you read the
cell death literature,

00:54:17.430 --> 00:54:20.010
there are a large number
of nucleases, each of which

00:54:20.010 --> 00:54:22.761
has been implicated in the
process of programmed cell

00:54:22.761 --> 00:54:23.260
death.

00:54:23.260 --> 00:54:25.320
In one study or another.

00:54:25.320 --> 00:54:27.630
So Gillian Stanfield
was a graduate student

00:54:27.630 --> 00:54:30.690
in the lab, cloned and
molecularly characterized

00:54:30.690 --> 00:54:33.090
nuc-1, and found
that indeed it does

00:54:33.090 --> 00:54:37.266
directly encode a nuclease, and
a nuclease of the sort DNase-2.

00:54:37.266 --> 00:54:39.390
Which made some people very
happy, and other people

00:54:39.390 --> 00:54:40.840
very disappointed.

00:54:40.840 --> 00:54:44.220
But, we could add, this
wasn't all there was to it,

00:54:44.220 --> 00:54:48.960
and studies that Julian did with
graduate student Yi-Chun Wu,

00:54:48.960 --> 00:54:53.280
she showed that in fact there
are at least three nucleases

00:54:53.280 --> 00:54:56.640
involved in this process, two
of which are not identified.

00:54:56.640 --> 00:54:59.620
The field is very happy.

00:54:59.620 --> 00:55:02.730
OK, so we take all
of this together,

00:55:02.730 --> 00:55:07.130
and we end up with today's
molecular genetic pathway

00:55:07.130 --> 00:55:09.170
for programmed cell death.

00:55:09.170 --> 00:55:14.480
Again, we have the four
general steps that I described.

00:55:14.480 --> 00:55:17.840
I've told you about
molecular, genetic,

00:55:17.840 --> 00:55:21.710
and developmental studies for
some, but certainly not all,

00:55:21.710 --> 00:55:23.390
of these players.

00:55:23.390 --> 00:55:26.390
But what I will say is
that essentially all

00:55:26.390 --> 00:55:29.880
of these players have
human counterparts.

00:55:29.880 --> 00:55:32.300
And insofar as
they've been analyzed,

00:55:32.300 --> 00:55:35.630
they seem to be involved in
the process of programmed cell

00:55:35.630 --> 00:55:36.784
death.

00:55:36.784 --> 00:55:38.450
Some of these have
counterparts that are

00:55:38.450 --> 00:55:40.890
associated with other diseases.

00:55:40.890 --> 00:55:43.790
For example, these two
genes here, ces-1 and ces-2,

00:55:43.790 --> 00:55:46.980
cell that specification,
have human counterparts

00:55:46.980 --> 00:55:51.770
that are associated with ALL,
acute lymphoblastic leukemia,

00:55:51.770 --> 00:55:53.640
another cancer.

00:55:53.640 --> 00:55:57.360
And there are other counterparts
through most of this pathway.

00:55:57.360 --> 00:56:02.210
So from this, we conclude
that much, and possibly

00:56:02.210 --> 00:56:07.300
all of this pathway, is
conserved amongst organisms

00:56:07.300 --> 00:56:12.350
as superficially different
as a microscopic worm and us.

00:56:12.350 --> 00:56:16.040
Now, I should say that if one
looks at what we know today

00:56:16.040 --> 00:56:18.500
about worms, and
what we know today

00:56:18.500 --> 00:56:23.180
about human programmed cell
death, mammalian more general,

00:56:23.180 --> 00:56:25.560
there are apparent differences.

00:56:25.560 --> 00:56:28.070
We don't know yet if
these differences are

00:56:28.070 --> 00:56:32.240
real biologic differences, or
if these differences are in part

00:56:32.240 --> 00:56:36.200
like the differences of the wise
men who expected the elephant,

00:56:36.200 --> 00:56:38.570
and one looked at the trunk,
and one looked at the tail,

00:56:38.570 --> 00:56:40.370
and one looked at
the leg, and had

00:56:40.370 --> 00:56:42.680
somewhat different impressions.

00:56:42.680 --> 00:56:48.790
We know we don't know at all,
we're looking to find out more.

00:56:48.790 --> 00:56:52.210
Let me put this, then,
in a broader perspective.

00:56:52.210 --> 00:56:56.950
I think, if one looks
at the major findings,

00:56:56.950 --> 00:56:59.230
maybe the major
finding, in the field

00:56:59.230 --> 00:57:03.540
of molecular genetics over
the last 5 to 10 years,

00:57:03.540 --> 00:57:07.140
what we as a field would
say is that what has really

00:57:07.140 --> 00:57:09.840
been striking is a
phenomenon that I

00:57:09.840 --> 00:57:12.840
like to refer to
as the principle

00:57:12.840 --> 00:57:15.590
of biologic universality.

00:57:15.590 --> 00:57:20.160
In other words, despite
the diversity of organisms,

00:57:20.160 --> 00:57:22.950
we share a lot in common.

00:57:22.950 --> 00:57:27.270
Worms and people, and
the fruit fly drosophila,

00:57:27.270 --> 00:57:32.960
and a yeast cell that's used
for baking bread or making beer.

00:57:32.960 --> 00:57:37.220
There are genes and gene
pathways in all of us

00:57:37.220 --> 00:57:38.540
that are strikingly similar.

00:57:38.540 --> 00:57:43.730
And we can learn about each
from studies of the other.

00:57:43.730 --> 00:57:48.490
And that, I think, maybe
I should say I thought,

00:57:48.490 --> 00:57:50.200
was the big news.

00:57:50.200 --> 00:57:53.980
Until I saw this.

00:57:53.980 --> 00:58:00.010
The cover of Punch
Magazine, from 1882,

00:58:00.010 --> 00:58:03.550
where clearly the
artist knew more

00:58:03.550 --> 00:58:10.060
than I did, until very recently,
in saying, man is but a worm.

00:58:12.630 --> 00:58:18.439
With that the people, members
of my laboratory over the years

00:58:18.439 --> 00:58:20.980
who have been involved in the
study of programmed cell death,

00:58:20.980 --> 00:58:24.040
current members involved
in that project,

00:58:24.040 --> 00:58:27.370
members of the lab involved in
a variety of projects today.

00:58:27.370 --> 00:58:30.220
And I actually would like to
ask the members of my lab, past

00:58:30.220 --> 00:58:34.420
and present who are here
today, please stand up.

00:58:34.420 --> 00:58:38.450
Because it's you who
are being recognized.

00:58:38.450 --> 00:58:41.922
[APPLAUSE]

00:59:13.740 --> 00:59:17.940
MODERATOR: Keeping to
an old tradition, when

00:59:17.940 --> 00:59:21.060
you give a talk, and
you give new data,

00:59:21.060 --> 00:59:26.280
and you express new
ideas, you take questions.

00:59:26.280 --> 00:59:30.420
Bob has done that, and Bob
deserves a couple of questions.

00:59:30.420 --> 00:59:33.356
We'll take a couple of
questions from the audience.

00:59:33.356 --> 00:59:35.348
Yes.

00:59:35.348 --> 00:59:39.332
AUDIENCE: [INAUDIBLE]
was talking about,

00:59:39.332 --> 00:59:41.822
but after hearing
him, I was wondering,

00:59:41.822 --> 00:59:46.810
how close are you to
practice this in human genes?

00:59:46.810 --> 00:59:50.137
HORVITZ: To-- the question
is how close are we

00:59:50.137 --> 00:59:50.845
to practice this?

00:59:50.845 --> 00:59:52.570
And the question is,
do you mean to use

00:59:52.570 --> 00:59:55.690
this information for
treating disease,

00:59:55.690 --> 00:59:58.730
or to study this process?

00:59:58.730 --> 00:59:59.750
To study this process.

00:59:59.750 --> 01:00:04.220
There are many studies
ongoing today in human beings,

01:00:04.220 --> 01:00:08.570
in other mammals, that are
drawing upon this information,

01:00:08.570 --> 01:00:12.020
and also deriving brand
new information in lots

01:00:12.020 --> 01:00:13.310
of exciting ways.

01:00:13.310 --> 01:00:15.629
So this is very
actively on-going.

01:00:21.990 --> 01:00:26.596
AUDIENCE: My question would
be, what turns those genes on

01:00:26.596 --> 01:00:29.230
to start the
process [INAUDIBLE]??

01:00:29.230 --> 01:00:30.980
HORVITZ: Right, what
turns those genes on?

01:00:30.980 --> 01:00:35.125
OK, that's my next half seminar.

01:00:35.125 --> 01:00:38.160
So, the pathway that
I told you about

01:00:38.160 --> 01:00:43.320
began with this gene egl-1,
the bcl-2-like killer gene.

01:00:43.320 --> 01:00:48.330
And in fact, we've shown
that this gene is regulated

01:00:48.330 --> 01:00:52.350
at the level of gene expression,
RNA synthesis, transcription.

01:00:52.350 --> 01:00:55.920
We know one of the
transcriptional regulators.

01:00:55.920 --> 01:01:00.930
And basically, it causes this
gene to be off in certain cells

01:01:00.930 --> 01:01:05.610
that should live, and when
that regulator is not active,

01:01:05.610 --> 01:01:07.180
and I can tell you
how that happens,

01:01:07.180 --> 01:01:10.170
but it's a longer story,
then this gene is on.

01:01:10.170 --> 01:01:14.880
So the specific action of this
one transcriptional regulator

01:01:14.880 --> 01:01:18.600
actually makes the decision
for one nerve cell type

01:01:18.600 --> 01:01:21.270
whether that cell
should live or die.

01:01:21.270 --> 01:01:24.390
For a second nerve
cell type, we have

01:01:24.390 --> 01:01:26.730
to transcriptional
regulators, one of which

01:01:26.730 --> 01:01:28.380
regulates the other.

01:01:28.380 --> 01:01:33.030
And we believe that the second
regulates, again, egl-1.

01:01:33.030 --> 01:01:35.500
Although we don't
have data for that,

01:01:35.500 --> 01:01:38.260
I think that's the way
the story is emerging.

01:01:38.260 --> 01:01:40.890
So the generalization
that we get

01:01:40.890 --> 01:01:44.860
from these small
number of studies,

01:01:44.860 --> 01:01:48.300
but which we and now
others are also pursuing,

01:01:48.300 --> 01:01:51.390
is that a key point
in the regulation

01:01:51.390 --> 01:01:54.470
here is at the level
of this gene egl-1,

01:01:54.470 --> 01:01:57.390
and that basically
in every cell there

01:01:57.390 --> 01:02:00.300
is some combination of
these transcription factors

01:02:00.300 --> 01:02:04.740
that decides whether or not
egl-1 will be expressed.

01:02:04.740 --> 01:02:08.340
If it's expressed, it makes
the gene, it makes the RNA,

01:02:08.340 --> 01:02:11.160
it makes the protein, the
protein interacts with CED-9,

01:02:11.160 --> 01:02:13.410
boom, boom, boom, cell dies.

01:02:13.410 --> 01:02:16.290
If it's not expressed, that
process doesn't initiate,

01:02:16.290 --> 01:02:19.020
cell survives.

01:02:19.020 --> 01:02:22.470
I could do the 60 minute
version of that too.

01:02:22.470 --> 01:02:23.790
MODERATOR: Is it really a--

01:02:23.790 --> 01:02:25.830
last question.

01:02:25.830 --> 01:02:27.950
AUDIENCE: Do you
think the absence,

01:02:27.950 --> 01:02:30.080
or too much
programmed cell death

01:02:30.080 --> 01:02:33.840
is a major contributor
to evolution?

01:02:33.840 --> 01:02:38.190
HORVITZ: It is very clear
that some evolutionary change

01:02:38.190 --> 01:02:42.060
has occurred by altering
patterns of programmed cell

01:02:42.060 --> 01:02:43.060
death.

01:02:43.060 --> 01:02:45.580
So that I'll give
you two examples,

01:02:45.580 --> 01:02:48.570
quickly, because I had
one up there already.

01:02:48.570 --> 01:02:51.000
When you look at
those p cell lineages,

01:02:51.000 --> 01:02:54.120
where we got those
green fluorescent cells,

01:02:54.120 --> 01:02:57.180
and I told you that
some cells survived

01:02:57.180 --> 01:03:00.300
and other cells
died, basically, when

01:03:00.300 --> 01:03:03.730
we think about the
evolution of cell lineage,

01:03:03.730 --> 01:03:05.820
we think about it
much the same way

01:03:05.820 --> 01:03:09.240
as we think about the
evolution of anything.

01:03:09.240 --> 01:03:13.920
Genes, segments in a segmented
animal like an insect.

01:03:13.920 --> 01:03:16.110
Basically, the way
evolution occurs

01:03:16.110 --> 01:03:19.770
is by a process of
duplication and modification.

01:03:19.770 --> 01:03:21.810
The same is true
of cell lineage.

01:03:21.810 --> 01:03:25.990
You duplicate a cell lineage,
and then you modify it.

01:03:25.990 --> 01:03:31.050
And in fact, in that case the
modification involves changes.

01:03:31.050 --> 01:03:33.780
Because not all of
the complements,

01:03:33.780 --> 01:03:36.080
all of the complement
of that set of six cells

01:03:36.080 --> 01:03:39.510
it's generated, are needed in
all of those different places

01:03:39.510 --> 01:03:41.080
where that lineage occurs.

01:03:41.080 --> 01:03:42.900
Some are needed, some are not.

01:03:42.900 --> 01:03:45.000
One of a number of
modifications that

01:03:45.000 --> 01:03:47.160
seems to have occurred
evolutionarily,

01:03:47.160 --> 01:03:49.500
is a modification of
programmed cell death,

01:03:49.500 --> 01:03:51.960
essentially eliminating
one of the cell types

01:03:51.960 --> 01:03:54.257
in that region of the animal
where it's not needed.

01:03:54.257 --> 01:03:56.340
And I'll leave it with one
example instead of two,

01:03:56.340 --> 01:04:00.000
because I can feel Phil
looking at me from here.

01:04:00.000 --> 01:04:02.370
MODERATOR: I want to
say one final thing.

01:04:02.370 --> 01:04:05.550
Only great institutions
can attract people

01:04:05.550 --> 01:04:08.010
like Bob Weinberg.

01:04:08.010 --> 01:04:10.962
[LAUGHTER]

01:04:17.850 --> 01:04:21.360
HORVITZ: And I would
agree with that.

01:04:21.360 --> 01:04:24.650
MODERATOR: People
like Bob Horvitz.

01:04:24.650 --> 01:04:27.590
And the students who work with
Bob, and the post-docs who've

01:04:27.590 --> 01:04:31.460
worked for Bob, to create
such beautiful science.

01:04:31.460 --> 01:04:35.000
Great institutions are
led by great people,

01:04:35.000 --> 01:04:38.720
and I want to thank Chuck
Vest for participating

01:04:38.720 --> 01:04:43.250
today and being such a strong
supporter of basic science

01:04:43.250 --> 01:04:45.800
and the activities of MIT.

01:04:45.800 --> 01:04:48.860
So I'd like us to
end by all standing,

01:04:48.860 --> 01:04:55.590
and giving a standing ovation
to Bob Horvitz and MIT.

01:04:55.590 --> 01:04:58.940
[APPLAUSE]

